now the manchester bombing inquiry  reported earlier this week douglas  murray has written this column about it  this week and he joins me now douglas  you've been following this for quite  some time  what jumped out at you in this report  well we learned a number of things  uh the inquiry finds  uh the number of occasions on which  uh salman abadi the suicide bomber  in question on the night of the ariana  grande concert  in 2017 the inquiry finds a number of  occasions when he could have been  identified  and should have been identified could  have been stopped  and should have been stopped now  inquiries like this seems to me  having read a lot of them in my time  always are going to find  faults it's inevitable there will be  intelligence faults  there will be security faults human  error  and more and and this is no different to  that extent  uh there's human error of a staggering  kind  the most obvious being uh two uh um  members of the police who went for a  kebab half an hour away  uh when they were meant to be at the  venue and then ate their dinner  in a secure room and  basically weren't doing their job uh  there were things like that but as i say  in my column this week  they're really disturbing thing about  this  inquiry by sir john saunders is  something that is revealed by a single  witness  who worked for one of the security  companies at the manchester arena on the  night  and can you tell us a bit more about  this was kyle lawler the  who is at the time 18 year old security  guard paid four pounds 26 per hour  yeah um i mean something should be said  about this by the way which is before  serious services you know these security  companies as we know from previous  incidents they had a sort of veneer of  security  but basically if anything serious  happens uh um  most of the time these companies are not  fit for purpose  uh so they add a sort of feeling of  security without actually providing it  and what i'm about to say as i say in  the piece i don't criticize  mr lawler for this he was a young man  and  i can't imagine what he has on his  conscience in relation to this but he  was at least  honest to the inquiry uh kyle lawler  says that like a number of other people  on the night  he noticed salman abedi he couldn't have  failed to notice him in a way because  salman abadi was hanging around  for more than an hour and a quarter by  the entrance exits  of the building he was inside the  building he was struggling  with a 32 kilogram rucksack  which we now know contained the bomb  and uh kyle lawler was one of the people  on the night who  noticed him thought there was something  up uh and he says  specifically that what he noticed about  mr a baby going backwards and forward  struggling with  his extremely heavy uh backpack  was he said he had a nervous reaction  uh whenever anyone looked at him and  seemed  fidgety now that's  uh not the important thing the important  thing is what kyle lawler said  after this he stated to the inquiry  that he was fearful of identifying mr  abadi  he said he was fearful of being branded  a racist  and feared that he would be in trouble  if he got it wrong  and as i say in my piece uh  one shouldn't blame kyle lawler first it  is  perhaps inevitable some people would but  i admire the fact he was honest about it  he was honest about the fact that  actually in modern britain  quite understandably he made the  decision  that if you see something you shouldn't  say something you should shut up  because there's far more to risk by  saying something  and being accused of being a racist than  there is of not saying something  and the bomb going off that's a  calibration that modern britain  has indoctrinated people into  for many many years and you can't just  indoctrinate  people out of it when i was reading the  report i was really quite struck by the  kind of strength of character that carl  lawler must  have i mean i'm not sure in his  situation he was somebody who i think  learned his trade via an online course  so  he had been given very little training  no real experience  yet it would have been very easy for him  just to pretend that he'd seen  nothing to not have anybody publicly  blame him for missing something because  i'm sure some people will say well if  only he'd blown the whistle  of course he's putting himself up there  for criticism  i imagine though he's doing this to make  a rather important point  that a lot of people in his position  would hesitate  to blow the whistle for the reasons that  he gives that  you might be branded discriminatory and  i guess the same is true for anybody  who's in the security industry because  when you stop somebody who looks  suspicious i guess you do need to ask  yourself  am i being biased here to what extent is  racial profiling  morally acceptable when it comes to  stopping people going through airports  through other  shopping arenas another this is a  dilemma we've had ever since 9-1-1  that's right in fact of course as you  know and we have an entire industry now  in britain as across the rest of the  western world  which says that we've not just got  biases but they're inbuilt in us they're  implicit in us  and we have to eradicate them we have to  question our alleged biases  we've got to do double takes all the  time  mentally this is a whole industry that  the the  vast swathes of government the private  sector and others have imbibed  and this isn't the reason why salman  obedie got into it  that's just one further manifestation of  the problem  that kyle lawler has so sadly put his  finger on  as you say you know fraser you know this  is a young guy  who underwent very little training he  said that he had no particular knowledge  there was going to be an incident on the  night which is true he couldn't have  known that  uh he could have known they should have  been told that the terrorism threat in  the uk was severe at the time it was  only shortly  after the attack in westminster uh  before of course the attack on london  bridge  but the the the terrorism uh  risk status was at severe but  again it's people much more senior  than this very junior online trained  uh security officer who i think have to  take some responsibility for getting  britain into the position  that you can't honestly identify  somebody who you think might be a  suicide bomber  for fear of basically detonating your  own career  and i mentioned in my piece several  examples of  high profile police officers in greater  manchester  who set the scene for that because as i  mentioned in the piece  one year almost exactly before the  manchester arena bombing  there was a test  exercise at the manchester shopping  center  in which the local um security services  the local  emergency services trained for basically  an eventuality  uncannily like the one on the night in  the manchester arena  it was to test the preparedness of the  first responders of the emergency  services  and the whole occasion was marred by a  single detail  which is that the actor playing the  suicide bomber on that occasion  at the manchester shopping center  shouted allahu akbar  or allah is greatest before detonating  the  fake device that then led to the play  act and the  attempt to prepare the emergency  services the whole exercise  was um forgotten about and massively  uh marred by this fact because there was  an immediate backlash  against the uh whole scenario  for the person playing the suicide  bomber shouting allahu akbar  and therefore totally appallingly  outrageously unforgivably linking the  possibility of a suicide bombing  with islamist extremism and all  of the local community leaders went off  on this  how dare you make this connection and  the local police  people like the greater manchester  police and crime commissioner tony lloyd  all  apologized for it they said it's  outrageous it's it's  marred and otherwise successful event  and  i think uh tony lloyd the greater  manchester police and crime commissioner  said  that this didn't add anything to the  event the shouting of allah  it just has the potential he said to  undermine the great community relations  we have  in greater manchester so this is the  position that people like  kyle lawler found themselves on the  night that there was a real-time suicide  bomber  really standing in front of them under  their noses struggling with a 32  kilogram  bomb they had been told by every major  policeman  in the area that you should do nothing  to link these things and that is why we  had effectively tr  trained people trained people out of  identifying  what was under their noses right at the  time as you say douglas the  threat level was number three and but  it's right now it's number two  substantial it seems in the last 20  years there's hardly been many times  where there hasn't been  a kind of a threat and yet so so you can  understand why the security guard didn't  quite know what  they wouldn't have been really expecting  one of these attacks but you also have  to work  wonder just how much you can really  expect  out of relatively junior people who are  i mean you know  you could see probably somebody senior  perhaps a soldier or something might be  able to  to work that thing out but even the  metropolitan police have had lots of  problems stop and search  they've been accused of racially  profiling black londoners theresa may  told them to stop it and it can be a  very difficult balance  to to get right because if you you go  too much and of course you end up  with a system where people of um asian  or middle eastern outlook get asked for  their id even in the house of commons  here a lot more than  than white so you can see why there is  concern of  of taking this a bit too far in certain  circumstances  well what happens here is that the  balance  swings too far in one direction  and we have to accept the fact that in  2017  uh the profile of a  suicide bomber at a pop concert  pretty much exactly fitted the profile  of salman abadi  a young radicalized male of  north african or middle eastern descent  this was pretty much the the likely  person it was not a woman it was not  a white woman uh it was a particular  profile and you might say well  you know you've got to check 20 people  who don't fit the profile in order to  allow yourself  to check one person who does fit the  profile  but as we saw on the night in manchester  these sorts of games are ones you can  treat yourself  to until it gets really serious and the  manchester arena bombing  was an unbelievable event and it should  never be forgotten  it's been to some extent i've always  said slightly brushed over i have to say  when you consider the amount of  attention that some  outrages get the extraordinary way in  which it's sort of been  you know brushed over almost from the  beginning we had the whole don't look  back in anger sort of thing about it uh  this whole don't draw any lessons out  from it  and by the way there are still lessons  we don't know we just don't know  answers to very major questions even  after the saunders report i  i give an example in my column this week  of something we still don't know  salman a baby's parents came to the uk  in the 1990s the father was a member of  the libyan islamic fighting group which  uh subsequently joined al qaeda  uh uh joined up with al-qaeda as an  affiliate the father of salman abedi  was in the libyan islamic fighting group  and they fell out with colonel gaddafi  in the 1990s very sadly  these people fell out with each other  and so britain  gave sanctuary to the baby parents and  that's how young salman abadi was born  in the uk he was born because we gave  his parents asylum and uh salman abadi  and his utterly reprehensible brother  uh um were the gifts we got in britain  for this i still can't find  after all this time and even after this  review any explanation from  any official in the uk as to why this  happened  why did we give them asylum what did we  gain  from it what exactly other than two  appalling sons did they give to this  country or bring to this country  nothing nada zilch and yet  nobody in authority has ever yet  given an explanation for this let alone  been held accountable for this  and this is one of the things i still  find absolutely astonishing in the  british discussion of this  we always get in the uk as i've written  at spectator many times  we always get these sort of slightly  fake debates you know  to my mind stop and search is a  relatively comfortable debate  compared to some of the other debates  that we ought to have  why was it that the obedie family were  in the uk  why was it that in the london bridge  attack only weeks later  two of the three attackers had as far as  i can understand  no legitimate cause for being in the uk  one who lied about his nationality was  found out to have lied and then  anyway wasn't deported from the uk and  the other was somebody who claimed  asylum from pakistan a few years ago  why was the uk giving asylum to somebody  from pakistan in the late 1990s or early  2000s  why was it that when somebody pretends  to be a moroccan turns out to be a  libyan  hacks at women's throats on london  bridge while shouting  that nobody's much interested in what  he's doing here in the first place  and all the crappy security supremos get  on to the comfortable debates  they get onto things like oh to what  extent is online radicalization an issue  because it's so much easier to talk  about these crappy  third-rate issues of what you can do  with google  and and so on it's so much easier than  just those  basic elementary questions there has  never been an asking of those elementary  questions  there still hasn't been and i think it  points  to the failures we still have even after  all these years in the uk  of properly discussing what is a serious  security threat  that's ongoing but one potential answer  there is that we gave him  i don't know the details but at the time  colonel gaddafi wasn't exactly running a  benign regime you could certainly see  why  um somebody might be fleeing in fear of  their life from that regime  like most european countries you can  definitely see that you can definitely  see that so  that's what you want to see in the  country that question is relatively easy  to answer  that that question should be relatively  easy to answer but there will be  probably thousands of other libyan  refugees who've  led constructive lives into the country  i'm not at all sure  that the british taxpayers money  is uh well spent in giving asylum  to jihadists who've fallen out with  other jihadists  i mean it's possible we could decide to  be the great uh  convening center some people would argue  we have been in recent decades  for the sort of you know intra-jihadist  fallout community but i don't think  that's what the british  public ever wanted i know it's something  we were never asked about  uh so so so yes there are explanations  and i'd like to hear them and i'd like  them to be stressed  by the officials i'd like them to  justify their decisions i'd like them to  explain to the british public  why it seems there are so many cases of  people who've gone on to commit  terrorist acts  who as far as i can see gave britain  nothing  and took in the case of salman abadi 22  young lives  [Music]  you

